  "definition": {
   "definition": "RBI defines commercial paper as an unsecured money-market instrument issued in the form of a promissory note. It may be issued by companies, NBFCs (including housing finance companies), InvITs, REITs, All India Financial Institutions and other bodies corporate with a minimum net worth of ₹100 crore, provided all fund-based facilities availed by the issuer are classified as Standard at the time of issue. CPs are issued at a discount to face value in dematerialised form, in minimum denominations of ₹5 lakh and multiples thereafter, with a tenor of not less than seven days and not more than one year, and must carry a minimum credit rating of A3. These rules are set out in the Master Direction — RBI (Commercial Paper and Non-Convertible Debentures of original or initial maturity up to one year) Directions, 2024.",
   "source_name": "RBI Master Direction — Commercial Paper and Non-Convertible Debentures Directions, 2024",
